The guidance: "A relation joins two entities... Add one for each relationship the note states." The note states reporting relies on ledger. So add relation: reporting service depends_on ledger service. Also possibly a fact: "As of 2025-06-24, the reporting service still relies on the ledger service." That is new dated information confirming continuation. I think adding the fact with date is reasonable and not contradicting.

Also "keep that in mind for the maintenance" — mentions maintenance but no durable info beyond that; it's a reminder, arguably speculative/no entity named "maintenance". Skip.

Aliases: "Reporting" -> "reporting service", "Ledger" -> "ledger service". The note uses short names "Reporting" and "Ledger" for existing entities "reporting service" and "ledger service". Add entity_aliases entries.

So output:

{
 "add_facts": [
  {
   "content": "As of 2025-06-24, the reporting service still relies on the ledger service.",
   "entities": ["reporting service", "ledger service"],
   "kind": "fact",
   "confidence": 0.9,
   "evidence": "Reporting still relies on Ledger"
  }
 ],
 "add_relations": [
  {
   "src": "reporting service",
   "dst": "ledger service",
   "rel_kind": "depends_on",
   "evidence": "Reporting still relies on Ledger"
  }
 ],
 "entity_aliases": [
  {"name": "Reporting", "canonical": "reporting service", "evidence": "Reporting still relies on Ledger"},
  {"name": "Ledger", "canonical": "ledger service", "evidence": "Reporting still relies on Ledger"}
 ]
}

